---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import MermaidDiagram from '../components/MermaidDiagram.astro';
import {
  systemDiagram,
  agentInteractionDiagram,
  hooksDiagram,
  commandsDiagram,
  scriptsDiagram,
  runtimeStateDiagram,
  safetyDiagram,
  dashboardDiagram,
} from '../data/diagrams';
import { commands, categories } from '../data/commands';
---

<DocsLayout title="How It Works" sectionLabel="Architecture" activeSlug="arch-visual" hideNav>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>How VibeCrew Works</h2>
    <p>VibeCrew is a system of interlocking parts: a plugin shell, specialized agents, lifecycle hooks, slash commands, bash scripts, and per-project runtime state. Select a tab to see how each part fits together.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- Tab navigation -->
  <div class="arch-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Architecture sections">
    <button class="arch-tab active" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="panel-plugin" data-tab="plugin">Plugin Structure</button>
    <button class="arch-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-agents" data-tab="agents">Sub-Agents</button>
    <button class="arch-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-hooks" data-tab="hooks">Hooks</button>
    <button class="arch-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-commands" data-tab="commands">Slash Commands</button>
    <button class="arch-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-skills" data-tab="skills">Skills Reference</button>
    <button class="arch-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-scripts" data-tab="scripts">Bash Scripts</button>
    <button class="arch-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-runtime" data-tab="runtime">Runtime State</button>
    <button class="arch-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-safety" data-tab="safety">Permissions &amp; Safety</button>
    <button class="arch-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-dashboard" data-tab="dashboard">Vibe Dashboard</button>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════ Plugin Structure ═══════ -->
  <div class="arch-panel active" id="panel-plugin" role="tabpanel">
    <div class="panel-intro">
      <h3>Plugin Structure</h3>
      <p>VibeCrew is a <strong>Claude Code plugin</strong> &mdash; an add-on that gives Claude Code new abilities. When you open a project with VibeCrew installed, Claude Code reads a manifest file and automatically loads agents, skills, hooks, scripts, MCP servers, and permissions. Everything lives inside the <code>claude-plugin-vibe-crew/</code> directory.</p>
      <div class="key-points">
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon purple">&#9632;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What it is</strong>
            <p>A self-contained folder with a <code>plugin.json</code> manifest that registers agents, slash commands, hooks, scripts, and MCP servers &mdash; everything the system needs in one install.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What problem it solves</strong>
            <p>Without a plugin, you'd manually configure each agent, permission rule, and automation script for every project. The plugin bundles everything into a single install.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <MermaidDiagram chart={systemDiagram} caption="Plugin components and how Claude Code loads them" />

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Plugin Components</h4>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>File / Directory</th><th>What It Does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Plugin Manifest</strong></td>
            <td><code>.claude-plugin/plugin.json</code></td>
            <td>Entry point that Claude Code reads on startup. Registers every agent, skill, hook binding, and MCP server so they load automatically.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>MCP Servers</strong></td>
            <td><code>.mcp.json</code></td>
            <td>Configures external tool servers (Context7, Chrome DevTools, Playwright, and conditionally Semgrep, Sentry, Supabase, Stripe, Vercel, Figma) that give agents web search, browser control, and service integrations.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Permissions</strong></td>
            <td><code>settings.json</code></td>
            <td>Allow/deny rules that control what agents can do without prompting. Allows file ops, npm scripts, and git; blocks sudo, force push, and destructive commands.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Hooks</strong></td>
            <td><code>hooks/hooks.json</code></td>
            <td>Event bindings that map Claude Code lifecycle events (session start, tool use, stop) to bash scripts that enforce rules at zero token cost.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Scripts</strong></td>
            <td><code>scripts/</code></td>
            <td>~80 bash programs that handle mechanical work: state mutations, scoring, git operations, notifications, formatting, and project analysis.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Agents</strong></td>
            <td><code>agents/</code></td>
            <td>System prompt files that define each specialized AI agent's role, tools, constraints, and output format.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Skills</strong></td>
            <td><code>skills/</code></td>
            <td>Slash command definitions as <code>SKILL.md</code> files. Each contains YAML metadata and a workflow body that routes to the right agent(s).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Templates</strong></td>
            <td><code>templates/</code></td>
            <td>Project scaffolding templates, doc-site scaffold, and an MCP registry of additional servers that can be auto-discovered from TDR technology choices.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Tests</strong></td>
            <td><code>tests/</code></td>
            <td>BATS test suite that validates hook scripts, state management, and core plugin behavior.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════ Sub-Agents ═══════ -->
  <div class="arch-panel" id="panel-agents" role="tabpanel" hidden>
    <div class="panel-intro">
      <h3>Sub-Agents</h3>
      <p>Instead of one AI doing everything, VibeCrew splits work across <strong>specialized agents</strong> &mdash; like a real development team with planners, builders, testers, and reviewers. Most agents run on Opus, the most powerful model available. This is intentional: while Opus costs more per task and takes longer, the higher quality output means significantly less rework. Getting it right the first time saves more time and money than running a cheaper model that needs multiple correction rounds. Fast, mechanical tasks (routing, running shell commands) use Haiku, and template-driven output like documentation uses Sonnet.</p>
      <div class="key-points">
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon purple">&#9632;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What they are</strong>
            <p>AI specialists with scoped tool access, model selection, and turn budgets. Most run on Opus (the most capable model) because planning, coding, security, and analysis are tasks where mistakes are expensive to fix later. Haiku handles fast mechanical work, and Sonnet handles documentation.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What problem they solve</strong>
            <p>A single AI context window fills up fast when researching, coding, and testing in one session. Worktree agents offload expensive research into isolated contexts &mdash; only the distilled output enters your main session, saving thousands of tokens.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="agent-legend">
      <span class="legend-item"><span class="legend-dot" style="background:var(--color-accent)"></span> Opus (complex reasoning)</span>
      <span class="legend-item"><span class="legend-dot" style="background:var(--color-amber)"></span> Haiku (fast tasks)</span>
      <span class="legend-item"><span class="legend-dot" style="background:var(--color-blue)"></span> Sonnet (documentation)</span>
    </div>

    <MermaidDiagram chart={agentInteractionDiagram} caption="Agent topology: how agents hand off work to each other" />

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Agent Reference</h4>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Agent</th><th>Model</th><th>Isolation</th><th>What It Does</th><th>Triggered By</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Session Startup</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge amber">Haiku</span></td>
            <td>Inline</td>
            <td>Environment check, state detection, handoff detection, routing</td>
            <td>Every session start</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Workflow Orchestrator</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Inline</td>
            <td>Routes between Tier 1 and Tier 2, coordinates agent handoffs, manages backlog</td>
            <td>Command routing</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Stack Scout</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Worktree</td>
            <td>Read-only research via WebSearch, Context7, Chrome DevTools. Produces TDR</td>
            <td>Tier 1 architecture step</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Builder</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Worktree</td>
            <td>Implements features: component selection, code generation, accessibility checks</td>
            <td>Tier 2 design + code phases</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Verifier</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge amber">Haiku</span></td>
            <td>Inline</td>
            <td>Runs tests, lint, build, and type-check. Reports pass/fail results</td>
            <td><code>/check</code>, <code>/wrap</code>, <code>/run-backlog</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Performance Coach</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Inline</td>
            <td>Cross-session trend analysis, anti-pattern detection, CLAUDE.md mutation proposals</td>
            <td><code>/wrap</code> session end</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Code Auditor</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Worktree</td>
            <td>Read-only codebase analysis for existing project onboarding</td>
            <td><code>/onboard</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Security Auditor</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Worktree</td>
            <td>OWASP Top 10 security analysis: injection, auth, XSS, secrets</td>
            <td><code>/audit</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Doc Generator</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge sonnet">Sonnet</span></td>
            <td>Inline</td>
            <td>Feature docs, CHANGELOG, release notes, VitePress sidebar regeneration</td>
            <td><code>/wrap</code>, Tier 2 docs phase</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Code Simplifier</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Worktree</td>
            <td>Dead code detection, abstraction flattening, API surface reduction</td>
            <td><code>/simplify</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>CI Healer</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Inline</td>
            <td>CI failure diagnosis and repair. Categorizes failures, max 3 fix attempts</td>
            <td><code>/heal</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Opponent Processor</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Worktree</td>
            <td>Devil's advocate for TDR decisions: counter-arguments, debate matrices, risks</td>
            <td>Tier 1 after TDR</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Code Reviewer</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Worktree</td>
            <td>Read-only code review against feature spec, TDR compliance, conventions</td>
            <td><code>/review</code>, <code>/run-backlog</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>System Reviewer</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Worktree</td>
            <td>Plugin meta-analysis, cross-project telemetry, ecosystem research</td>
            <td><code>/system-review</code></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════ Hooks ═══════ -->
  <div class="arch-panel" id="panel-hooks" role="tabpanel" hidden>
    <div class="panel-intro">
      <h3>Hooks</h3>
      <p>Hooks are <strong>automatic safety checks</strong> that run behind the scenes at zero token cost. When an agent tries to write a file or run a command, hooks intercept the action and validate it before it executes. Think of them as security guards at every door &mdash; they check permissions instantly using small bash scripts, not AI processing.</p>
      <div class="key-points">
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon purple">&#9632;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What they are</strong>
            <p>Bash scripts triggered by Claude Code lifecycle events (session start, before/after tool use, session end). They read a JSON payload from stdin and return exit code 0 (allow) or 2 (block).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What problem they solve</strong>
            <p>AI models can forget rules mid-session. Hooks enforce constraints deterministically &mdash; the phase gate blocks source code writes before the foundation is complete, the quality gate catches build errors, and the data protector blocks destructive commands. No token cost, no forgetting.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <MermaidDiagram chart={hooksDiagram} caption="Hook events mapped to their enforcement scripts" />

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Session Init Hooks</h4>
    <p class="cmd-group-desc">Run automatically when a new Claude Code session starts.</p>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Script</th><th>Event</th><th>What It Does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>session-startup.sh</code></td>
            <td>SessionStart</td>
            <td>Checks environment (Git, Node, jq), detects terminal type, loads state, and routes to the right workflow.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>sync-state.sh</code></td>
            <td>SessionStart</td>
            <td>Reconciles state files on startup &mdash; ensures <code>.vibecrew/</code> JSON is consistent after crashes or manual edits.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>error-recovery.sh</code></td>
            <td>SessionStart</td>
            <td>Clears stale lock files and repairs corrupted state from previous interrupted sessions.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>compact-reinject.sh</code></td>
            <td>SessionStart (compact)</td>
            <td>Re-injects essential context (active feature, phase, conventions) after Claude Code's <code>/compact</code> clears the context window.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Pre-Action Guards</h4>
    <p class="cmd-group-desc">Intercept actions before they execute. Return exit code 2 to block.</p>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Script</th><th>Event</th><th>What It Does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>phase-gate.sh</code></td>
            <td>PreToolUse (Write/Edit)</td>
            <td>Blocks source code writes until the Tier 1 foundation (VISION, TDR, architecture, CLAUDE.md) is complete. Prevents coding before decisions are made.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>restrict-paths.sh</code></td>
            <td>PreToolUse (Write/Edit)</td>
            <td>Blocks file writes outside the project root directory. Prevents agents from modifying system files or other projects.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>protect-data.sh</code></td>
            <td>PreToolUse (Bash)</td>
            <td>Blocks destructive shell commands: <code>rm -rf</code>, <code>DROP TABLE</code>, <code>DELETE FROM</code> without WHERE, force push, and privilege escalation.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>validate-phase-transition.sh</code></td>
            <td>PreToolUse (Bash)</td>
            <td>Enforces phase ordering &mdash; ensures foundation steps run in sequence and artifacts are complete before advancing.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>validate-signal.sh</code></td>
            <td>PreToolUse (Write/Edit)</td>
            <td>Validates inter-agent signal file schemas, enum values, and feature ID cross-references before they're written.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Post-Action Processors</h4>
    <p class="cmd-group-desc">Run after an action completes successfully.</p>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Script</th><th>Event</th><th>What It Does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>format-code.sh</code></td>
            <td>PostToolUse (Write/Edit)</td>
            <td>Auto-formats files after every write using the project's configured formatter (Prettier, ESLint, etc.).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>notify.sh</code></td>
            <td>Notification / PostToolUseFailure</td>
            <td>Sends native OS notifications for permission stalls, task completions, and errors. Includes Warp Terminal deep-links when available.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Session End Checks</h4>
    <p class="cmd-group-desc">Run when a session stops to catch issues before they persist.</p>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Script</th><th>Event</th><th>What It Does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>quality-gate.sh</code></td>
            <td>Stop</td>
            <td>Runs typecheck, lint, and build on modified source files. Blocks task completion if any check fails &mdash; the agent must fix errors before finishing.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>claude-md-lint.sh</code></td>
            <td>Stop</td>
            <td>Validates CLAUDE.md file size and structure. Keeps it under the recommended line limit to avoid context bloat.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>cost-guardrails.sh</code></td>
            <td>Stop</td>
            <td>Tracks session and daily API costs against configured thresholds. Warns or blocks when spending exceeds limits.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>check-context.sh</code></td>
            <td>Stop</td>
            <td>Monitors context window usage and warns at 60% and 80%. Helps prevent sessions from running out of context mid-task.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════ Slash Commands ═══════ -->
  <div class="arch-panel" id="panel-commands" role="tabpanel" hidden>
    <div class="panel-intro">
      <h3>Slash Commands</h3>
      <p>Slash commands are the <strong>primary user interface</strong> to VibeCrew. You type a command starting with <code>/</code> and the system handles the rest. Each command is defined as a <code>SKILL.md</code> file with YAML frontmatter (name, description, arguments) and a markdown body containing the full workflow: preconditions, steps, error handling, and agent handoffs.</p>
      <div class="key-points">
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon purple">&#9632;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What they are</strong>
            <p>Commands across several categories: setup, planning, development, quality, safety, session management, and gamification. Some are script-only (zero token cost), others orchestrate multiple agents.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What problem they solve</strong>
            <p>Without slash commands, you'd need to manually prompt the AI with detailed instructions for every workflow step. Commands encode expert workflows into one-word triggers &mdash; <code>/wrap</code> runs quality checks, scores your session, generates docs, and commits, all automatically.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <MermaidDiagram chart={commandsDiagram} caption="How a slash command flows from user input to results" />

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Setup &amp; Onboarding</h4>
    <p class="cmd-group-desc">Run these once when starting a new project or joining an existing one.</p>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What It Does</th><th>When to Use</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>/setup</code></td>
            <td>Verifies dependencies (Git, Node, jq), detects your terminal, and creates the <code>.vibecrew/</code> folder.</td>
            <td>First thing you run in any project. Tells you if anything is missing.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>/new-project</code></td>
            <td>Runs the full 7-step foundation: VISION.md, design system, TDR, architecture diagrams, roadmap, and CLAUDE.md.</td>
            <td>Starting a brand-new project from scratch. This is the Tier 1 workflow.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>/onboard</code></td>
            <td>Analyzes an existing codebase &mdash; extracts conventions, tech stack, and generates a CLAUDE.md.</td>
            <td>Adding VibeCrew to a project that already has code.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>/profile</code></td>
            <td>8-question interview (or preset selection) that configures how agents communicate and work with you.</td>
            <td>After setup, to personalize agent behavior for your experience level and preferences.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Planning</h4>
    <p class="cmd-group-desc">Define what to build and track progress.</p>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What It Does</th><th>When to Use</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>/plan-features</code></td>
            <td>Interactive session to define backlog specs: problem statement, acceptance criteria, priority, complexity, dependencies.</td>
            <td>After foundation is complete, to define what features you want to build.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>/idea "text"</code></td>
            <td>Captures a one-line idea to <code>backlog.json</code> with status "unplanned". Zero AI cost.</td>
            <td>Anytime you have a feature idea &mdash; quick capture without breaking your flow.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>/status</code></td>
            <td>Full project dashboard: foundation progress, backlog overview, active feature, recent Vibe Scores.</td>
            <td>To see where your project stands at a glance. Script-only, no token cost.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>/sync-issues</code></td>
            <td>Imports open GitHub/GitLab issues by label into the VibeCrew backlog.</td>
            <td>To pull external issue tracker items into VibeCrew for execution.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Development</h4>
    <p class="cmd-group-desc">Build features and fix bugs.</p>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What It Does</th><th>When to Use</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>/new-feature "name"</code></td>
            <td>Creates a branch, checkpoint, and starts the 6-phase development cycle (Plan &rarr; UI Design &rarr; Code &rarr; Test &rarr; Review &rarr; Docs).</td>
            <td>To start building a planned feature. One feature at a time (WIP limit enforced).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>/run-backlog</code></td>
            <td>Automatically executes features from the backlog by priority. Stops on failure for human review.</td>
            <td>When you want VibeCrew to work through multiple features autonomously.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>/check</code></td>
            <td>Runs tests, build, lint, and type-check without modifying any code.</td>
            <td>Quick health check at any time during development.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>/debug</code></td>
            <td>Structured debugging with hypothesis testing, variable inspection, and root cause analysis.</td>
            <td>When something is broken and you need a methodical investigation.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>/fix-issue &lt;number&gt;</code></td>
            <td>Fetches a GitHub/GitLab issue, diagnoses the problem, implements a fix, tests it, and creates a PR/MR.</td>
            <td>To fix a specific issue end-to-end from your issue tracker.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

